Aristotle: association, citizenship, and a good community (@greek-aristotle-politics: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-plato-aristotle, #source @greek-source-politics, #philosophy @greek-aristotle-poetics, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-aristotle-poetics) { r_0NZK4NKSQVX9NMTAWRT2XNBATD
**Read:** [Politics, published in this translation as A Treatise on Government](../sources/Politics.lingua#r_1DZ4505WSQF0FN2EDX5WT9W4PX), translated by William Ellis. Begin with Book I, chapters 1–7, and Book III, chapters 1 and 6–9. Book VII, chapters 1–3 supplies a further comparison concerning the good life and the community's purpose.

**Preparation:** [reasons for civic obligation](../socratic/Crito.lingua#r_54N10WGKCSCKW6XM202PCG7ZDH) and [Aristotle's account of living well](Living-well-and-character.lingua#r_21YB00AWNRYQ0X2SQ8H0RFRW65) are useful comparisons. Introduce household, citizen, constitution, and common advantage through the passages themselves.

Ask what makes an association political, who is counted as a citizen, and how the proposed end of the association justifies its arrangements. Examine the argument about natural slavery: identify its criteria and the inference from a proposed human difference to an authority over another person. Compare what the account describes with what it claims is justified.

**Practice:** reconstruct one argument about membership, authority, or the common advantage. Identify a person whose work or situation is relevant but whose participation is restricted in the account. Explain what would have to change in the justification to include that person.

**Check:** distinguish a definition of citizenship, an explanation of an institution, and a defence of its legitimacy. Compare the account with [Republic](../plato/Republic.lingua#r_05G1N50PP71GRGDNAPG38CQ8J5) or [Protagoras on civic education](../socratic/Protagoras.lingua#r_6QKC211R791MTAMXSZM0VBS5XN) using passages from both.

**Crossings:** ethics, education, work, political economy, and the distribution of roles. **Return:** [Plato and Aristotle](../Plato-and-Aristotle.lingua#r_6Z4HA576BYZD1JVJ66TJE0QQJE) · [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).
